last_agent_message:
You can preview them directly in PowerShell on Windows.

Run these one at a time:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -Command "[System.Media.SystemSounds]::Asterisk.Play()"
powershell -NoProfile -Command "[System.Media.SystemSounds]::Beep.Play()"
powershell -NoProfile -Command "[System.Media.SystemSounds]::Exclamation.Play()"
powershell -NoProfile -Command "[System.Media.SystemSounds]::Hand.Play()"
powershell -NoProfile -Command "[System.Media.SystemSounds]::Question.Play()"
```

`none` just means no sound.

If you want a quick tester, this plays all of them in sequence with labels:

```powershell
$pairs = @(
  @{ Name = 'asterisk';   Cmd = { [System.Media.SystemSounds]::Asterisk.Play() } }
  @{ Name = 'beep';       Cmd = { [System.Media.SystemSounds]::Beep.Play() } }
  @{ Name = 'exclamation';Cmd = { [System.Media.SystemSounds]::Exclamation.Play() } }
  @{ Name = 'hand';       Cmd = { [System.Media.SystemSounds]::Hand.Play() } }
  @{ Name = 'question';   Cmd = { [System.Media.SystemSounds]::Question.Play() } }
)
foreach ($pair in $pairs) {
  Write-Host $pair.Name
  & $pair.Cmd
  Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
}
```

If you want, I can also set your preferred completion sound and prompt sound permanently in the Startup launcher.
